  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 318 PM EDT Fri Sep 4 2026\n\n- Showers and Thunderstorms This Evening\n\nThe radar this afternoon shows scattered thunderstorms in an area\nof isentropic ascent and moisture advection from southern \nWisconsin into southwest Lower Michigan. The convective-allowing \nmodels such as the hi-res ARW and FV3 show the storms across \nsouthern Wisconsin moving east-southeast this evening and \ntraversing the southern half of the forecast area. This is \nreflected in the forecast, although tweaks may be needed of course\nthanks to the inherent uncertainty in modeling convection that we\nhave seen the past several days.\n\n- Drying out and cooling off this Weekend\n\nA developing Omega block across the CONUS this weekend results as\nthe central U.S. heat dome is bookended by cut off lows across\nthe NE and NW CONUS. This lowers heights across the central Great\nLakes and allows surface high pressure to build in from the\nnortheast with lower temperatures and dew points and a very fine\nLabor Day weekend from Saturday through Monday. In face, morning\nlows on Sunday and Monday could be in the upper 40s to lower 50s\nacross the northern half of the forecast area.\n\n- Rain Possible Tuesday into Wednesday\n\nThe surface high retreats east eastward by Tuesday as the Omega\nblock breaks down. The low across the Pacific NW kicks east and\nflattens the upper high, with surface low pressure tracking across\nthe northern Great Lakes. Return flow of moisture and a surface\nwarm front should kick off some showers and thunderstorms late\nTuesday into Wednesday. Another round of storms will be possible\nas a cold front moves through late Wednesday.",
